Gentoo/Alpha Status Report
=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=
=3D

1. Status Reports
-----------------

Preliminaries

This is the status of the Gentoo/Alpha porting team. It will be posted=20
regularly, but not with a static frequency. All questions can be posted=20
to gentoo-alpha@gentoo.org. The Gentoo/Alpha porting team, has its own=20
project page (just like almost all other Gentoo projects). You can find=20
it at http://alpha.gentoo.org/. Also you can contact us via IRC at=20
#gentoo-alpha on irc.freenode.net. The latest status report can always=20
be found on the Gentoo Linux Alpha Status Reports[1] subproject page.

[1] http://www.gentoo.org/proj/en/base/alpha/status/index.xml

Index

This status report will briefly discuss the following tasks, objectives,=20
and/or projects related to the Gentoo/Alpha porting team:

    * New Developers
    * Keywording and Stabilization
    * Firefox and Thunderbird are Broken
    * Documentation Project
    * Alpha Arch Testers Project
    * New Alpha project page
    * Donations
    * 2.6 SMP Kernels

2. Content
=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D

New Developers
--------------

Since the last status report, the following developers have joined the=20
Gentoo/Alpha porting project:

    * Christel Dahlskjaer (christel)
      Christel joined the Gentoo/Alpha porting team recently to help=20
      with testing kernels on SMP hardware.
    * Thomas Cort (tcort)
      Thomas joined the Gentoo/Alpha porting team in early April to help=20
      with bug fixing, keywording, documentation, and security bugs.
    * Chris Gianelloni (wolf31o2)
      Chris joined the Gentoo/Alpha porting team recently to help with=20
      testing kernels on SMP hardware.

Keywording and Stabilization
----------------------------

Stephen Bennett (spb) has continued his work with SELinux on alpha.=20
hardened-sources is now keyworded for alpha. Thanks to the work of=20
Stefaan De Roeck (stefaan) and others, modular X has been keyworded and=20
is working well on alpha. The Alpha arch team is also pleased to=20
announce that we have stabilized gnome-2.12.3 and kde-3.5.2 since the=20
last status report.=20

Firefox and Thunderbird are Broken
----------------------------------

The mozilla project[2] has chosen to discontinue the development of the=20
1.0.x versions of firefox and thunderbird. Since that decision, multiple=20
security holes have been uncovered. As a result, we had to mask the=20
1.0.x versions of firefox and thunderbird to keep our users secure since=20
there are no plans to fix the security holes.

No one on the Gentoo/Alpha team has been successful at getting the 1.5.x=20
versions of firefox and thunderbird to run correctly, see Bug #113944=20
and Bug #131359. If you can help get these packages working on alpha, it=20
would be very much appreciated.=20

[2] http://mozilla.org/
[3] https://bugs.gentoo.org/113944
[4] https://bugs.gentoo.org/113944

Documentation Project
---------------------

The documentation subproject has written multiple new documents:

    * The Gentoo/Alpha FAQ[5] - an FAQ intended to answer some of the=20
      most frequently asked questions relating to Gentoo/Alpha and=20
      Linux/Alpha in general.
    * Alpha Porting Guide[6] - a guide intended to show C programmers=20
      how to port programs written on other architectures to the Alpha=20
      architecture.

Thomas Cort (tcort) has begun work on an SRM console guide. It will be=20
published in the documentation subproject[7] section of the Gentoo/Alpha=20
project page.=20

[5] http://www.gentoo.org/doc/en/gentoo-alpha-faq.xml
[6] http://www.gentoo.org/proj/en/base/alpha/doc/alpha-porting-guide.xml
[7] http://www.gentoo.org/proj/en/base/alpha/doc/index.xml

Alpha Arch Testers Project
--------------------------

The Alpha Arch Testers Project was recently created by Fernando J.=20
Pereda (ferdy). The project is meant to help the Alpha Arch Team provide=20
a stable, secure and up to date Gentoo Linux port by allowing users to=20
participate in the testing and stabilization process. If you want to=20
learn more about this excellent opportunity to give back to Gentoo,=20
please check out the Alpha Arch Testers Project Page[8].=20

[8] http://www.gentoo.org/proj/en/base/alpha/AT/index.xml

New Alpha Project Page
----------------------

Our project page was significantly revamped by Jose Luis Rivero=20
(yoswink) and Fernando J. Pereda (ferdy) with the help of the rest of=20
the Gentoo/Alpha team. The developer list has been refreshed to reflect=20
the current team membership. We now have information about our=20
subprojects too. Most subprojects now have their own project pages.

    * Gentoo/Alpha Project Page[9]

[9] http://alpha.gentoo.org

Donations
---------

Hardware donations are always welcome. We will gladly accept spare alpha=20
hardware. Even an old AlphaServer 1000 is useful for testing=20
installation media and kernels. More powerful systems could be used to=20
assist in testing packages, to provide developers with access to alpha=20
hardware to port applications, or to assist in building releases. Alpha=20
computers aren't the only hardware that we could benifit from; a SCSI or=20
IDE DVD burner would allow us to test dvdrtools, PCI 802.11 NICs would=20
allow us to do more extensive testing and unmask the wifi USE flag=20
(currently only 1 dev has an alpha with wireless), PDAs would allow us=20
to test PDA applications and unmask the pda USE flag. If you would like=20
to make a donation, please drop by #gentoo-alpha on irc.freenode.net or=20
send an e-mail to alpha@gentoo.org.

2.6 SMP Kernels
---------------

The Linux kernel developers have been working hard on the Alpha port.=20
SMP is functional again and has been tested by a number of developers=20
and users in #gentoo-alpha. 2.6.15.1 and 2.6.16.19 have both been tested=20
on SMP hardware with good results.

